---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("verification");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
  <section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview" data-search="verify release download checksums signatures provenance">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Releases and Verification</p>
    <h1>
      Verify what you download.<span>Know what it proves.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Download only from <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/releases">official GitHub Releases</a>. The sandbox image is
      <code>ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox</code>. Verify downloaded artifacts before executing them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Provenance identifies a build's source and workflow. It does not prove the code is harmless, that the maintainer account was uncompromised, or that a scan
      will detect every risk.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="current-evidence-status">
    <h2>Release evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Verification results apply only to the exact release and source commit. Inspect the matching release assets and workflow run, then verify your download
      below.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="version-and-commit-identity">
    <h2>Version and commit identity</h2>
    <p>
      Run <code>repyy version</code> only after verifying the downloaded artifact. It reports version, full source commit, builder label, fixed source
      repository, Go version, build date and rules version. Missing values are <code>unknown</code>; local builds label their builder
      <code>development</code>. These strings are diagnostics, not cryptographic identity. Official workflow builds inject metadata into binaries and the
      sandbox image.
    </p>
    <p>
      Local scan reports cannot claim an immutable checkout commit: local content may be uncommitted or change during inspection. Remote reports include the
      fetched revision. Report conversion preserves the original scanner identity rather than substituting the converter's version.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="verify-provenance">
    <h2>Verify provenance</h2>
    <p>
      The configured release workflow runs from <code>main</code> after its CI check and builds the tagged source. For releases containing{" "}
      <code>release.json</code>, use the <a href="#fresh-download-release-gate">fresh-download gate</a>. It first verifies the record's attestation against the{" "}
      <code>release.yml@refs/heads/main</code> identity, resolves the tag to its actual source commit, and checks the record's tag, source commit and complete
      public-file inventory before verifying artifacts. The record also names the controller commit, allowing pipeline repairs without retagging the source.
      Standard GitHub attestations identify the main workflow; the verified record binds files to tagged source. This is configured policy, not evidence that a
      later release passed.
    </p>
    <p>
      For legacy releases without <code>release.json</code>, use a recent GitHub CLI with the <code>gh attestation</code> command. For a downloaded archive or
      extracted binary, replace the placeholders with the exact release and filename:
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>gh attestation verify ARTIFACT --repo Kevin-Umali/repyy --cert-identity https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --source-ref refs/tags/vX.Y.Z
</code></pre>
    <p>
      Require the expected repository, workflow and release ref. Check the source commit in the verification output against the release notes. Attestations for
      extracted binaries let users verify after unpacking; archives and Linux packages also receive provenance.
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/use-artifact-attestations/use-artifact-attestations">GitHub's attestation guide</a>
      explains verification and available policy controls.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="checksums-and-existing-signatures">
    <h2>Checksums and existing signatures</h2>
    <p>
      A checksum detects changed bytes only after the checksum list itself is authenticated. Releases configured from v0.7.1 use Cosign 3 keyless signatures in{" "}
      <code>checksums.txt.sigstore.json</code>, which includes the signature, certificate, and transparency-log evidence.
    </p>
    <p>For a legacy release with a Cosign 3 bundle, use its tag-based identity:</p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>cosign verify-blob checksums.txt --bundle checksums.txt.sigstore.json --certificate-identity https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
sha256sum --check checksums.txt
</code></pre>
    <p>
      For older releases, replace <code>--bundle checksums.txt.sigstore.json</code> with
      <code>--signature checksums.txt.sig --certificate checksums.txt.pem</code> and download both files. Keep the same expected workflow identity and OIDC
      issuer.
    </p>
    <p>
      On macOS, use <code>shasum -a 256 --check checksums.txt</code>. Windows users can verify individual hashes with <code>Get-FileHash</code>, after
      authenticating the checksum list. Do not treat a filename or download location alone as verification.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="sbom-access-and-scope">
    <h2>SBOM access and scope</h2>
    <p>
      Container inventories are published separately as <code>repyy-container-linux-amd64.spdx.json</code> and
      <code>repyy-container-linux-arm64.spdx.json</code>. Each is attested against its platform image digest, recorded in the corresponding
      <code>.txt</code> asset. These platform digests are selected from the signed multi-platform image index; an inventory for one architecture does not claim
      coverage of the other. Per-archive <code>*.sbom.json</code> assets identify archive contents. <code>repyy-release.spdx.json</code> describes the release
      build directory as a set, including platform binaries and packages. It is not a separate per-platform dependency assertion. The workflow binds that set
      inventory to each binary/archive/package digest using an SPDX attestation. Read its package inventory and relationships rather than interpreting an SBOM
      as a vulnerability scan.
    </p>
    <p>For a legacy release, verify an SPDX attestation with the tag-based identity:</p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>gh attestation verify ARTIFACT --repo Kevin-Umali/repyy --cert-identity https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --source-ref refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --predicate-type https://spdx.dev/Document
</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="container-verification">
    <h2>Container verification</h2>
    <p>
      Read the exact image digest from the verified <code>repyy-sandbox-image.txt</code> asset. Replace <code>DIGEST</code> and the version below for a legacy
      release without <code>release.json</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:DIGEST --repo Kevin-Umali/repyy --cert-identity https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --source-ref refs/tags/vX.Y.Z
cosign verify ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:DIGEST --certificate-identity https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/vX.Y.Z --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
</code></pre>
    <p>Pull that digest explicitly after verification. A digest pins bytes; signing/provenance identifies who built them. Neither supplies VM isolation.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="fresh-download-release-gate">
    <h2>Fresh-download release gate</h2>
    <p>Run the release workflow's Linux verification script from a Repyy checkout. Choose a download directory that does not exist yet.</p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>bash scripts/verify-release.sh vX.Y.Z /tmp/repyy-verification-vX.Y.Z
</code></pre>
    <p>
      Install GitHub CLI with attestation support, Cosign, Python 3, and SHA-256 tools. The script downloads the release assets and checks their signatures,
      hashes, provenance, and SBOM attestations. A failed check blocks publication in CI.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="repository-security-signals">
    <h2>Repository security signals</h2>
    <p>
      Inspect <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/actions/workflows/ci.yml">CI runs</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/actions/workflows/codeql.yml">CodeQL runs</a>, the{" "}
      <a href="https://scorecard.dev/viewer/?uri=github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy">Scorecard breakdown</a>, and
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/attestations">artifact attestations</a> for the relevant commit. A passing workflow does not establish a
      clean security assessment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Branch and tag protection and review requirements are live GitHub settings. Check them in GitHub; a workflow file alone cannot enforce them. No
      independent audit or certification is claimed.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
